  "findings": [
    {
      "claim": "The unit does not show that it fixes item AU, and its own measurement points away from the passes it changed. The brief says to measure first and name the pass that burns, because the steward runners were using 54 to 63 percent of a core continuously. The commit bounds the alert (health) and worker-census reads and changes the sleep. But its measurement shows those passes take about 5 to 21 ms (health) and about 270 ms (census) per tick, and the census figure is only that high because the fixture makes every record 1 MiB. At the default 10-minute tick, that is under 0.1 percent of a core. The loop before this commit already slept the full interval after every tick. So caching these reads and sleeping 'interval minus work' (which sleeps slightly less than before) cannot remove a continuous 60 percent load. Whatever burns the core is unnamed and still there.",
      "evidence": "Traced path. (1) internal/steward/runner.go TickSeconds returns 600 when no config is set; the real checkout has no metasystem.steward.tick-seconds override. (2) In the base commit, both the helm path and the normal path ended in runnerWait(top, interval, ...). That loop sleeps 200 ms per step until start+interval and returns early only on a stop request or a provider-limit change, so the old runner already idled between ticks. (3) The commit's TestRunnerPassCost (runner_idle_test.go:420-437) logged, on this machine: '211 open alerts, 267 proof payloads (1 MiB each): health alert read=20.9ms census supplement=271ms'. (4) The real stores the cache covers hold 0 to 33 small files (alerts 248 KB in total, mains 68 KB), not 1 MiB records. (5) The new wait (runner.go, wait closure) sleeps interval-work, which is never longer than the old sleep. Result: neither change can account for, or remove, a continuous 54 to 63 percent load. The per-tick timing line is useful instrumentation, but the brief's 'name the pass that burns' and the item's outcome ('a steward runner must idle between ticks') are not delivered. The burn is likely in per-cycle work outside the two bounded passes (bridge Step, TrimCaches, DeliverPending, the channel phase, or a background goroutine), and none of that was measured.",
      "id": "F-1",
      "material": true,
      "severity": "high"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The per-tick timing line makes runner.log grow without limit. One existing reader, logOrphanSeatRun, reads the whole file on every orphan report to avoid duplicates, so that reader's cost now grows with every tick. That contradicts the spirit of 'a tick's cost does not grow', but at the default 10-minute cadence the growth is about 10 KB a day, so neither the slice's function nor its safety depends on fixing it. This reader must appear in the unit's readers list; that list could not be checked (see gaps).",
      "evidence": "internal/steward/seat_idle.go:201-229 opens runner.log and runs ReadString over every line until the end of the file. The new runner.go logTick appends one line per tick. No Go code rotates runner.log.",
      "id": "F-2",
      "material": false,
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The timing line can mix two clocks. The 'health' time is measured with the tick's health-dependencies clock, while the 'work' and 'other' times use the runner loop's clock. In production both are the wall clock, so the line is correct there. In fixtures, 'other' can be skewed or negative, and the public-verb test shows 'health 0.000ms'.",
      "evidence": "tick.go completeTickHealthWithDependencies uses dependencies.now() for healthElapsed. runner.go logTick computes other = work - result.healthElapsed - timed.elapsed, with work taken from deps.Now().",
      "id": "F-3",
      "material": false,
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Possible staleness, not demonstrated. The snapshot cache trusts the directory's modification time for up to 10 ticks (100 minutes at the default cadence). On a filesystem with coarse timestamps (for example ext4's jiffy granularity), an atomic rename could land in the same timestamp step as the snapshot and go unseen until the 10-tick audit. On the APFS hosts in use, timestamps have nanosecond resolution and every writer found publishes atomically, so there is no reproduction or traced path on the deployed platform. Recorded as noted.",
      "evidence": "internal/steward/runner_reads.go:712 returns the cached entries when the directory's SameFile and ModTime match and state.tick-previous.tick < runnerReadAuditTicks (10). The writers found (atomicfile.WriteText, WriteVolatileFile, saveAlertEpisode) all use temp-file-plus-rename.",
      "id": "F-4",
      "material": false,
      "severity": "low"
    }
